# .gitignore — Whis Android Project

# ── Gradle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.gradle/
build/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

# ── Android / IDE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.iml
.idea/
*.hprof
local.properties
*.log
captures/

# ── Signing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.jks
*.keystore

# ── OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Firebase ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The PLACEHOLDER google-services.json is tracked in VCS so the build
# always succeeds. However, when a developer drops in the REAL
# google-services.json (with actual Firebase credentials), we must NOT
# let it get committed — that would leak the Firebase config if this
# repo goes public.
#
# Workflow:
#   1. The placeholder is already committed and tracked.
#   2. After replacing it with the real file, run:
#        git update-index --assume-unchanged app/google-services.json
#      This tells Git to ignore local changes to the file while keeping
#      the placeholder in the repo history.
#   3. To re-track changes (e.g., to update the placeholder):
#        git update-index --no-assume-unchanged app/google-services.json
#
# We do NOT gitignore the file outright because that would remove the
# tracked placeholder from VCS, breaking fresh clones.
